{'num': 7, 'title': 'Stage 4 - Review and Stage-Gate Approvals',
 'blocks': [
  ('p', 'Stage gates are the quality checkpoint between doing the work and accepting it. A gate is a formal review of a deliverable - or a completed Waterfall phase - routed through the approval chain defined in workflow configuration, typically a Team Lead tier followed by the Project Sponsor. Gates protect the sponsor\'s accountability, create a dated acceptance record for every major deliverable, and in Waterfall projects unlock the next phase for time logging. Until the final gate tier approves, the deliverable formally remains "in review" regardless of how finished it feels.'),
  ('h2', '7.1  Submitting a Stage-Gate Review (PM)'),
  ('p', 'When a deliverable or phase is ready, the Project Manager opens the Approvals tab and submits a gate review. The submission bundles the evidence the chain needs: the deliverable or phase reference, the milestone it satisfies, completion notes and links to the underlying tasks with their logged hours. The first tier in the gate chain is notified immediately, and the submission appears in the Approvals tab with its per-tier status.'),
  ('nav', 'Projects / (project) / Approvals / Submit stage-gate review'),
  ('steps', [
    'Confirm the deliverable\'s tasks are Done and timesheets against them are submitted.',
    'Open Approvals and press Submit stage-gate review; pick the deliverable or phase and the milestone it closes.',
    'Write completion notes the sponsor can actually rely on - scope covered, tests or acceptance evidence, known gaps.',
    'Submit. Tier 1 of the gate chain is notified; track progress in the Approvals tab.',
  ]),
  ('h2', '7.2  The Team Lead to Sponsor Chain'),
  ('p', 'The chain executes tier by tier, strictly in order. The Team Lead (or first tier) reviews the engineering reality - are the tasks genuinely done, is the work quality acceptable - and approves or rejects with comments. Approval advances the chain and notifies the next tier. The Project Sponsor reviews at the acceptance level - does this satisfy the business objective - and their approval is the consequential one: on the final tier\'s approval the platform stamps the linked milestone as approved with its actual date, which is precisely what closure later reads. A rejection at any tier returns the submission to the PM with the reviewer\'s comments; rework happens in Stage 3 and the gate is resubmitted from scratch.'),
  ('img', '07-approvals.png', 'Figure 7-1: The Approvals tab - gate submissions with per-tier status; your actionable tiers are highlighted'),
  ('h2', '7.3  What Approvers See and Do'),
  ('p', 'Approvers act from the Approvals tab or the notification link. Tiers where you are the current decision-maker are visually highlighted as actionable; tiers where you already decided show the decision and date. Decisions are final for that submission round - there is no recall, only a new submission after rework. Everything (who decided, when, with what comments) is preserved in the project\'s approval trail on the Activity feed, which is what auditors and the closure readiness report read.'),
  ('callout', 'note', 'Gate approvals unlock Waterfall phases',
   'In Waterfall projects, the gate approval that closes Phase N is also the key that unlocks Phase N+1 for time logging. If your team reports "cannot log time" on a new phase, check whether the previous phase\'s gate is still pending before assuming a system fault.'),
 ]},

# ══════════════════════════ CHAPTER 8 ══════════════════════════
{'num': 8, 'title': 'Stage 5 - Closure and Post-Mortem',
 'blocks': [
  ('p', 'Closure is a controlled landing, not an off switch. The Closure tab computes a live readiness report, collects the post-mortem that feeds the Performance module, and only then allows the compliance sign-off that formally closes the project - sealing allocations, archiving boards and fixing the final financial picture. A project that cannot show its evidence stays open.'),
  ('h2', '8.1  The Closure Readiness Report'),
  ('p', 'The readiness report is generated from live data the moment you open the Closure tab. It states, without anyone hand-compiling it: total hours logged on the project; actual cost computed from employee cost rates (stored encrypted and decrypted only for authorised viewers); budget variance against the project budget and per-phase caps; milestone variance - what percentage of milestones hit their approved dates; task statistics (completed versus cancelled versus open); and open compliance items from the closure checklist. Each item is present or outstanding, so the gap to closure is always a list, never a mystery.'),
  ('nav', 'Projects / (project) / Closure'),
  ('img', '09-closure.png', 'Figure 8-1: The Closure tab - readiness metrics, post-mortem editor and compliance sign-off'),
  ('h2', '8.2  Post-Mortem Reviews and Performance Feedback'),
  ('p', 'Before sign-off, every confirmed project member is rated on the post-mortem form across three dimensions: delivery quality, communication and speed, each on a simple rating scale, plus a free-text contribution note. Separately, the PM records skill upgrade suggestions - "Priya should add Terraform to her master skill list" - which notify HR to update the employee\'s skill inventory so future matchmaking benefits. The contribution metrics and ratings synchronise to the Performance module, where they become evidence in the employee\'s annual appraisal cycle. This is the loop that makes project history compound: today\'s delivery data is tomorrow\'s matching, staffing and appraisal input.'),
  ('steps', [
    'Open the post-mortem panel on the Closure tab.',
    'Rate each confirmed member on delivery quality, communication and speed; add a one-line contribution note.',
    'Record skill upgrade suggestions where the project revealed new capabilities.',
    'Submit the post-mortem - HR is notified of skill suggestions immediately.',
  ]),
  ('h2', '8.3  Compliance Checklist and Sign-off'),
  ('p', 'The compliance checklist (defined by your administrators in workflow configuration) enumerates what "done" means: typically all stage gates approved, all milestones stamped, timesheets fully approved and no pending submissions, the post-mortem complete for every member, and budget variance acknowledged. The sign-off button activates only when the checklist is satisfied and the readiness report shows no outstanding items. Signing off requires the authority of a Tenant Admin or the executive sponsor; the signer\'s name and the closure date are stamped permanently on the project.'),
  ('callout', 'warning', 'Sign-off is final',
   'Closure seals the project: allocations are closed, the board is archived read-only, and no further timesheet rows are accepted against it. If any billing reconciliation is outstanding, complete it before signing - reopening a closed project requires an administrator and is discouraged.'),
  ('h2', '8.4  After Closure'),
  ('p', 'A closed project remains fully readable: the workspace tabs keep their final state, the readiness report freezes as the closure record, and the project card on the Projects page shows the Completed badge with final progress. Its data continues to work - hours feed revenue and utilisation reports, the post-mortem ratings surface in appraisals, skill suggestions refresh the inventory, and the project appears as a reference point in future matchmaking context. The project\'s collaboration room can be retained or archived by the Collaboration module\'s own lifecycle rules.'),
 ]},

