# ══════════════════════════ CHAPTER 11 ══════════════════════════
{'num': 11, 'title': 'FAQ and Troubleshooting',
 'blocks': [
  ('p', 'Most support questions in this module trace back to a governance rule working as designed. The answers below name the rule, explain where the message comes from, and give the shortest path forward. If a symptom is not listed, check the Activity tab first - nearly every automated decision is logged there with its reason.'),
  ('h3', 'Q1. Why can I not log time on my Waterfall task?'),
  ('p', 'The phase gate is closed: the task belongs to a phase whose predecessor has not passed stage-gate approval. The rejection message names the blocking phase. The fix is procedural, not technical - have the PM submit ( or chase ) the previous phase\'s gate review; approval unlocks logging immediately.'),
  ('h3', 'Q2. Why can I not start the next sprint?'),
  ('p', 'The one-active-sprint rule is in force: a project pod runs exactly one active sprint. Complete the current sprint on the Sprints tab (unfinished stories sweep to the backlog or the next sprint automatically), then start the next one.'),
  ('h3', 'Q3. My card will not move to In Progress - the drag snaps back or shows a warning. Why?'),
  ('p', 'The task has an unfinished dependency. The dependency chip on the card is amber and marked "(gate)" while the blocker is open. Finish the blocker task (or edit the task to remove the depends-on link if the constraint no longer applies), then move the card.'),
  ('h3', 'Q4. Matchmaking returned few or weak candidates. What now?'),
  ('p', 'The engine scored your requirement against real skills and live availability. Widen the requirement (drop one must-have skill, widen the start window), or check the Capacity Planner - if the right people are at or above the over-allocation threshold, no scoring can conjure capacity. When the strict skill pool is empty the engine says it fell back to the general bench pool; treat those results as "nearest available", not "best skilled".'),
  ('h3', 'Q5. Who approves my timesheet, and why did it go to the "wrong" person?'),
  ('p', 'Routing follows the configured chain: the approver on your project allocation first, the project\'s PM as fallback, the admin queue as last resort. If your allocation\'s approver field is empty or stale, the PM receives it. Ask your PM to set the allocation approver correctly - future rows then route automatically.'),
  ('h3', 'Q6. Submitting intake keeps failing validation. What is missing?'),
  ('p', 'The intake validator enforces exactly what your administrators marked mandatory in workflow configuration - commonly the business objective, the executive sponsor or the target completion date. The inline message names the field. Admins can also see the toggles in Section 1 of Workflow Configuration.'),
  ('h3', 'Q7. The project was approved - where is the chat room and board?'),
  ('p', 'Workspace automation runs on final approval if enabled. Check the "Automated trigger status" panel on the Overview tab: it lists the chat room, the delivery board and member notifications with their created state. If your tenant disabled automation, create the surfaces manually in the Collaboration module and link them from the workspace.'),
  ('h3', 'Q8. Why is someone flagged at 130 percent in the Capacity Planner?'),
  ('p', 'Their confirmed plus soft allocations across projects exceed the configured threshold (default 100 percent) for those weeks. Open the employee chip to see the two competing allocations; release one, shorten it, or - in genuine emergencies - have an admin raise the threshold, knowing the alert will then stay quiet at your peril.'),
  ('h3', 'Q9. Leave was approved - why are project managers suddenly notified?'),
  ('p', 'The backup hand-off rule fired: an allocated member\'s approved absence reached the configured trigger (default five working days), so PMs of the member\'s active projects are asked to arrange cover. Assign or soft-reserve a backup; the planner\'s pre-check helps confirm the backup has room.'),
  ('h3', 'Q10. What is the difference between a soft and a confirmed allocation?'),
  ('p', 'Soft is a bench reservation: it blocks the person\'s capacity and appears on their calendar but commits nobody. Confirmed activates the membership with project role and notifications, adds them to assignee pickers, and routes their timesheets. Convert soft to confirmed through the Resources tab once the reporting manager releases the person.'),
  ('h3', 'Q11. Can I delete a task that already has logged hours?'),
  ('p', 'Deleting performs a soft-cancel: the task leaves the active board but its timesheet rows are preserved so billing history stays intact. If you truly need to purge history, that is an administrator action and is discouraged - cancelled state plus audit trail is the intended record.'),
  ('h3', 'Q12. Where do post-mortem ratings actually go?'),
  ('p', 'Two places: the contribution metrics flow into the Performance module as appraisal evidence, and skill upgrade suggestions notify HR, who update the employee\'s master skill inventory. Both loops immediately influence future matchmaking scores - the system literally remembers who delivered.'),
 ]},

